---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import DocPage from '../../components/docs/DocPage.astro';
import { theorems, deferred } from '../../data/formalization';
import { LEAN_URL, THEOREM_MAP_URL, LEAN_STATUS_URL, TESTS_URL } from '../../consts';

const title = 'Formalization — DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'Ten LEAN 4 theorems, each bound to an executable Rust witness, including the counterexample proving that partial trace does not preserve commutation. Plus the seven targets that carry test witnesses and no proof yet.';

const TESTS = 197;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/formalization/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <DocPage
    eyebrow="Evidence"
    title="What is proved, and what is not"
    lede="The verifiable path is backed by LEAN 4. Ten theorems close with no sorry, each tied to a Rust test that runs the same statement on real numbers."
    sourceHref={LEAN_URL}
    sourceLabel="lean/DeepCausalityFormal/Quantum"
  >
    <h2>Why these theorems</h2>

    <p>
      The Mathlib version the workspace pins carries neither a partial trace nor
      a Choi–Jamiołkowski layer, so both are built from first principles over a
      pair-indexed matrix model. That is more work than importing them would
      have been, and it fixes what can be proved: the results below are about
      the reconstruction, not about a library someone else validated.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every theorem is bound to a Rust witness through
      <a href={THEOREM_MAP_URL}>THEOREM_MAP.md</a>, and the witnesses live in
      <a href={`${TESTS_URL}/formalization_lean`}>tests/formalization_lean</a>.
      The binding runs both ways in practice: a theorem with no witness is a
      statement nobody exercises, and a witness with no theorem is a test whose
      generality is unknown.
    </p>

    <h2>The headline is a refutation</h2>

    <div class="callout callout-warn">
      <p class="callout-title">partial_trace_preservation is false</p>
      <p>
        Partial trace is positive and linear, and it is not an algebra
        homomorphism. The counterexample gives two matrices with
        <code>[X, Y] = 0</code> whose partial traces do not commute. The
        residual commutator is <code>[[0, 4], [−4, 0]]</code>, which is
        <code>+4i·σy</code>. Because that is an integer matrix, the proof closes
        by <code>decide</code> over ℤ, with no analysis and no
        <code>sorry</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      What survives is the conditional statement. A boundary operator of the
      form <code>Z ⊗ 1_B</code> that commutes with <code>M</code> does force
      <code>Z</code> to commute with <code>Tr_B(M)</code>, and that is proved as
      <code>partial_trace_preservation_boundary</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Commutativity is therefore checked where the factors meet, by embedding
      both onto the union of their supports. Separately, the crate supports flat
      models only: the physical meaning of quantum-subgraph nesting is itself
      unestablished, so more work needs to be done.
    </p>

    <h2>Proved</h2>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Identifier</th>
            <th scope="col">Statement</th>
            <th scope="col">LEAN</th>
            <th scope="col">Rust witness</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {theorems.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code class="tid">{t.id}</code></td>
              <td class="tst">{t.statement}</td>
              <td><code class="tsrc">{t.lean}</code></td>
              <td><code class="tsrc">{t.witness}</code></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="after">
      All {theorems.length} close with zero <code>sorry</code>. The quantum tree
      is exempt from the repository's CI <code>sorry</code> gate while this
      foundation is being extended, which is recorded in
      <a href={LEAN_STATUS_URL}>LEAN_QUANTUM.md</a> rather than left implicit.
    </p>

    <h2>Stated, not proved</h2>

    <p>
      These carry numerical or property-test witnesses in the crate today. Their
      LEAN proofs need machinery the pinned Mathlib does not have.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Target</th>
            <th scope="col">What exists today</th>
            <th scope="col">Why not yet</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {deferred.map((d) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code class="tid">{d.id}</code></td>
              <td class="tst">{d.carries}</td>
              <td class="tst">{d.reason}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Tests</h2>

    <p>
      <code>cargo test -p deep_causality_quantum --all-features</code> runs
      {TESTS} tests and reports zero failures and zero ignored. There are no doc
      tests; the suite is behavioural and lives under <code>tests/</code>,
      mirroring the source tree file for file.
    </p>

    <h2>Scope of the faithfulness claim</h2>

    <p>
      Faithfulness is limited to the C₃-exclusion regime of van der Lugt and
      Lorenz. A declared causal structure with no C₃ sub-relation is faithfully
      representable by a traditional circuit, and one containing a C₃ is
      rejected at freeze. The general routed and direct-sum hypothesis of
      Lorenz and Barrett remains open upstream, and the crate makes no claim
      about it.
    </p>
  </DocPage>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  th, td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  th { color: var(--fg-0); font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }
  tbody tr:hover { background: var(--bg-1); }
  .tid { font-size: var(--t-mono-sm); color: var(--fg-0); background: none; padding: 0; }
  .tst { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: 34ch; }
  .tsrc { font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); color: var(--fg-1); background: none; padding: 0; }
  .after { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
</style>
